WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:11.680
Here is a puzzle. Two vesicles, each filled with GFP, sit 150 nanometres apart.

2
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.580
In the best ordinary light microscope, they look like one blob.

3
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.880
Zoom in, and the blob only gets bigger.

4
00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:22.220
Why can you not split them?

5
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:25.000
The reason is a property of light.

6
00:00:25.883 --> 00:00:29.743
Think of a rubber stamp with a soft, round face.

7
00:00:30.603 --> 00:00:35.163
However small the point you aim at, the mark is the size of the stamp.

8
00:00:35.943 --> 00:00:38.323
A lens does the same thing to light.

9
00:00:38.983 --> 00:00:43.003
The image of one point is a bright disc with faint rings.

10
00:00:43.543 --> 00:00:45.443
That is called an Airy pattern.

11
00:00:46.243 --> 00:00:50.163
Two marks closer than about one stamp width run together.

12
00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:55.887
Two glowing points give two patterns, and their intensities add.

13
00:00:56.887 --> 00:01:02.427
We call the points resolved when the centre of one pattern falls on the first dark

14
00:01:02.427 --> 00:01:03.467
ring of the other.

15
00:01:04.347 --> 00:01:06.227
That spacing is the resolution.

16
00:01:07.247 --> 00:01:12.707
It depends on the emission wavelength, and on the numerical aperture The numerical

17
00:01:12.707 --> 00:01:16.567
aperture measures how wide a cone of light the lens gathers.

18
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:22.300
Take light of 520 nanometres, like the glow of GFP.

19
00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:27.200
Use an oil lens with a numerical aperture of 1 .4.

20
00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:34.080
The resolution is 0 .61 times 520, divided by 1 .4.

21
00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:37.860
That is 226 .6 nanometers.

22
00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:44.260
A weaker lens, with a numerical aperture of 1 gives 317 .2.

23
00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:48.400
Losing a little aperture costs about 90 nanometres.

24
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:51.380
Now put the vesicles back.

25
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:57.120
They are 150 nanometres apart, closer than 226 .6.

26
00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:01.520
Their two blurs overlap and add into one spot.

27
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.700
That single merged spot is all the camera records.

28
00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:10.620
Enlarge the picture, and the spot spreads over more screen pixels.

29
00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:12.740
It adds no new information.

30
00:02:13.783 --> 00:02:19.043
Super-resolution gets around the limit by controlling which molecules glow.

31
00:02:19.903 --> 00:02:24.823
Switch on a sparse few at a time, so that no two neighbours glow together.

32
00:02:25.783 --> 00:02:30.763
The centre of each blur can be found far more precisely than the blur is wide.

33
00:02:31.643 --> 00:02:34.083
Plot the positions from thousands of cycles.

34
00:02:35.243 --> 00:02:38.803
Locating one molecule is not the same as resolving two.

35
00:02:38.803 --> 00:02:44.383
Because neighbours glow at different times, molecules closer than the limit can be told apart.

36
00:02:44.383 --> 00:02:46.803
Remember the rule.

37
00:02:47.563 --> 00:02:50.883
Magnification without resolution is empty magnification.
